WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.241 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Two classes both have a mode of two library books.

00:00:03.241 --> 00:00:05.795 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Must their borrowing patterns look the same?

00:00:05.795 --> 00:00:11.282 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Imagine each pupil placing one counter above the number of
library books they borrowed.

00:00:11.282 --> 00:00:15.607 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
As counters gather into stacks, some numbers may be more
common than others.

00:00:15.607 --> 00:00:19.240 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Before naming a most common value, look at the whole
arrangement.

00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:22.194 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
What story might those stacks tell about the class?

00:00:22.194 --> 00:00:27.666 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Today we are learning to describe the mode and shape of a
data distribution in context.

00:00:27.666 --> 00:00:31.955 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our goal is to describe the mode and the whole distribution
in context.

00:00:31.955 --> 00:00:37.649 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We want to say what the most common value means, but also
notice pupils whose values are different.

00:00:37.649 --> 00:00:42.204 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A single summary is a useful clue, rather than the entire
investigation.

00:00:42.204 --> 00:00:45.818 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can identify the most frequent value or category.

00:00:45.818 --> 00:00:49.475 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can describe clusters, gaps and how frequencies are
spread.

00:00:49.475 --> 00:00:52.848 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can compare two distributions using evidence.

00:00:52.848 --> 00:00:56.651 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Remember that frequency means how often a value occurs.

00:00:56.651 --> 00:01:02.209 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
In a column graph, the horizontal labels name the book
counts and the vertical scale counts pupils.

00:01:02.209 --> 00:01:04.936 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Read both labels before giving an answer.

00:01:04.936 --> 00:01:09.012 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Two books and three pupils describe different parts of the
same display.

00:01:09.012 --> 00:01:14.464 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The mode is the value or category with the highest
frequency, not the frequency itself.

00:01:14.464 --> 00:01:17.410 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Two values can tie for the highest frequency.

00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:22.026 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
If all observed values occur equally often, there is no
useful mode.

00:01:22.026 --> 00:01:25.466 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A distribution shows how all the responses are spread.

00:01:25.466 --> 00:01:31.031 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Describe where most values gather, any gaps and whether
counts are balanced or trail to one side.

00:01:31.031 --> 00:01:33.938 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Keep zero-frequency counts to show gaps.

00:01:33.938 --> 00:01:36.929 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Build the first display one pupil at a time.

00:01:36.929 --> 00:01:41.943 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Place one counter at zero books, one at one book, then three
counters at two books.

00:01:41.943 --> 00:01:44.795 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Finally place one at three and one at four.

00:01:44.795 --> 00:01:49.119 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We can replace each stack by a column without changing its
frequency.

00:01:49.119 --> 00:01:52.723 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Now the concrete counters and the graph show the same data.

00:01:52.723 --> 00:01:56.255 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Books borrowed: 0, 1, 2, 2, 2, 3, 4.

00:01:56.255 --> 00:01:58.827 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
What is the mode, and what does it mean?

00:01:58.827 --> 00:02:01.985 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Before hearing the answer, compare the column heights.

00:02:01.985 --> 00:02:05.253 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Which book-count value belongs to the tallest column?

00:02:05.253 --> 00:02:12.263 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Think about how you would finish this sentence: the most
common number of books is something, because something
pupils borrowed that many.

00:02:12.263 --> 00:02:15.107 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Keep the value and its frequency separate.

00:02:20.107 --> 00:02:23.843 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Two books is the mode because three pupils borrowed two
books.

00:02:23.843 --> 00:02:28.490 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Most values gather around two; the frequencies on either
side are balanced.

00:02:28.490 --> 00:02:31.219 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The mode is 2 books, not 3 pupils.

00:02:31.219 --> 00:02:35.347 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Count the three pupils at two books, then trace down to the
label two.

00:02:35.347 --> 00:02:37.171 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
That label is the mode.

00:02:37.171 --> 00:02:42.840 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
One pupil borrowed zero, and another borrowed four, so it
would be wrong to say everyone borrowed two.

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:47.739 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The distribution is balanced around two, with matching
frequencies on the two sides.

00:02:47.739 --> 00:02:52.794 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Class A frequencies for 0,1,2,3,4 books: 1,2,5,2,1.

00:02:52.794 --> 00:02:55.583 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Class B: 0,0,5,4,2.

00:02:55.583 --> 00:02:57.151 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
What stays the same?

00:02:57.151 --> 00:03:00.799 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Now compare two classes with eleven pupils each.

00:03:00.799 --> 00:03:05.761 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
For zero through four books, Class A has frequencies one,
two, five, two and one.

00:03:05.761 --> 00:03:08.949 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Class B has zero, zero, five, four and two.

00:03:08.949 --> 00:03:11.132 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Predict what the modes will share.

00:03:11.132 --> 00:03:15.093 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Then decide whether the rest of the distributions must
match.

00:03:20.093 --> 00:03:22.090 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Both have a mode of 2 books.

00:03:22.090 --> 00:03:23.957 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A is balanced around 2.

00:03:23.957 --> 00:03:28.194 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
B has no pupils at 0 or 1 and all values are 2 to 4.

00:03:28.194 --> 00:03:31.628 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A shared mode does not describe the whole distribution.

00:03:31.628 --> 00:03:34.929 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
If two bars tie at the top, report both modes.

00:03:34.929 --> 00:03:38.028 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Class A has pupils on both sides of two books.

00:03:38.028 --> 00:03:43.460 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Class B has no pupils at zero or one, and all its
observations are two, three or four.

00:03:43.460 --> 00:03:46.913 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Both classes have the same tallest value at two books.

00:03:46.913 --> 00:03:51.328 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Their shared mode does not make the two sets of observations
identical.

00:03:51.328 --> 00:03:55.399 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Use the empty positions and the other column heights as
evidence.

00:03:55.399 --> 00:03:59.799 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
“The mode describes everyone.” It describes the most common
value.

00:03:59.799 --> 00:04:03.444 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Look at all the bars before making a claim about a group.

00:04:03.444 --> 00:04:06.976 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Imagine one extra pupil reporting four books.

00:04:06.976 --> 00:04:12.944 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The highest numerical value has changed in frequency, but
that alone does not make four the mode.

00:04:12.944 --> 00:04:15.160 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Compare all frequencies again.

00:04:15.160 --> 00:04:20.816 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A mode is chosen by how often a value occurs, rather than by
how large the value is.

00:04:20.816 --> 00:04:24.871 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Pause the player and try the investigation in the written
lesson.

00:04:24.871 --> 00:04:29.473 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Make a prediction, collect or change the data, then explain
what you notice.

00:04:29.473 --> 00:04:31.521 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Try a tied distribution.

00:04:31.521 --> 00:04:37.843 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
If one book and two books each have five pupils, and every
other frequency is smaller, both are modes.

00:04:37.843 --> 00:04:44.856 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
If all the displayed possible values have the same
frequency, no single value stands out as a useful mode.

00:04:44.856 --> 00:04:48.699 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Report what the graph shows instead of forcing a single
answer.

00:04:48.699 --> 00:04:51.263 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Change the frequencies in the explorer.

00:04:51.263 --> 00:04:56.448 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Describe a cluster or gap and explain why the mode alone
misses part of the story.

00:04:56.448 --> 00:05:02.216 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
In the explorer, change one frequency and predict what will
happen before updating the display.

00:05:02.216 --> 00:05:04.934 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Can you create a gap with zero pupils?

00:05:04.934 --> 00:05:06.727 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Can you create two modes?

00:05:06.727 --> 00:05:10.309 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Can you keep the mode at two while changing the other
values?

00:05:10.309 --> 00:05:14.401 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Explain how your change affects the description of the whole
class.

00:05:14.401 --> 00:05:17.949 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A good report connects numbers to the people and the
question.

00:05:17.949 --> 00:05:23.755 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Which feature would you describe after giving the mode: a
gap, a cluster, or how the values are spread?

00:05:23.755 --> 00:05:31.165 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Use a specific book count and frequency to support your
explanation, and remember to look at every part of the
distribution.
